i {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/country"} "Medals by country"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/athlete"} "Medals by athlete"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal_table"} "Medal tables"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/participation"} "Participations"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/records"} "Olympic Records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age"} "Age records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age_at_death"} "Age at death"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/lists"} "Lists"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/today"} "Today"]] "\n" [:li {}] "\n" [:li {:class "divider"}] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics"} "Statistics"]] "\n"] "\n"] "\n\n\n\n\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/feedbacks/new"} "Feedback"]] "\n" [:form {:class "navbar-form navbar-left", :role "search", :action "/athletes/quick_search", :accept-charset "UTF-8", :method "post"} [:input {:name "utf8", :type "hidden", :value "✓", :autocomplete "off"}] [:input {:type "hidden", :name "authenticity_token", :value "xYDRUfpI2HyvfeQclAVIhVFEiMcgtPgtl40J4tG7zYWGVqBFtXzLt4K04cjjdnaCZhJYPviO8gqX7O_9BCnKhA", :autocomplete "off"}] [:div {:class "form-group"} "\n" [:input {:type "text", :name "query", :id "query", :class "form-control", :placeholder "Athlete search"}] "\n"] "\n" [:input {:type "submit", :name "commit", :value "Go", :class "btn btn-default", :data-disable-with "Go"}] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "container"} "\n" [:ol {:class "breadcrumb"} "\n" [:li {} "\n" [:a {:href "/"} "Home"] "\n"] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/editions"} "Games"]] [:li {:class "active"} "1928 Summer Olympics Overview"] "\n"] "\n\n\n" [:h1 {} "1928 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:h2 {} "Facts"] "\n" [:table {:class "biotable"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition type"] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic Games"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Number and Year"] "\n" [:td {} "IX / 1928"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Host city"] "\n" [:td {} "\nAmsterdam, Netherlands\n(" [:a {:href "/venues/editions/9"} "Venues"] ")\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n28 July\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n12 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n17 May – 12 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "OCOG"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/organizations/356"} "Nederlands Olympisch Comité (Comité 1928)"]] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/9"} "3296"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/9/countries"} "46 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n125\nin 20 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Other events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n3\nin 3 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The 1928 Olympics were an unusual event in that no single athlete dominated. " [:a {:href "/athletes/67728"} "Paavo Nurmi"] " was back and he won three more medals, but only " [:a {:href "/results/57705"} "one of them was gold"] ". " [:a {:href "/athletes/51611"} "Johnny Weissmuller"] " was back and again won two gold medals. The 1928 Olympic Games began with a minor controversy, when the Netherlands’ Queen Wilhelmina refused to attend the Opening Ceremony, reportedly because she was upset about not having been consulted on the date of the opening. The Games were instead opened by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100008"} "His Royal Highness Prince Hendrik"] ", but the Queen did attend the closing ceremony. During the Games, there was no Olympic Village, and none was necessary, because many of the teams boarded their athletes on ships moored in Amsterdam Harbor."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The biggest story of the 1928 Olympics was probably the emergence of women. The ancient Olympic Games did not allow women as competitors, or even as spectators. If they were found to be watching, they were supposedly put to death. " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Baron Pierre de Coubertin"] " originally did not support women competing in the Olympics, and thus, in the modern Olympics, women were admitted slowly and only grudgingly."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In 1900, women competed in " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/CQT"} "croquet"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/40001"} "golf"] ", " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/TEN"} "tennis"] ", " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/SAL"} "sailing"] ", and probably " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/ARC"} "archery"] ". In 1904, women competed only in " [:a {:href "/editions/3/sports/ARC"} "archery"] ". In both of those years, the events were hard to define, and women’s participation was never officially accepted or approved by the " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] ". That was to come in 1912, when " [:a {:href "/editions/6/sports/SWM"} "women’s swimming"] " was admitted to the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Track &amp; field athletics has always been the showcase sport of the Olympics. Women were not allowed to compete until 1928, not that they didn’t try. Track &amp; field is controlled by the " [:a {:href "/organizations/6"} "IAAF"] " (then the International Amateur Athletic Federation) and they did not support the admission of women’s track &amp; field to the Olympics. So, the women formed a separate organization, the{ FSFI (Fédération Sportive Féminine Internationale)](/organizations/310). The FSFI held its own events, the Women’s “Olympics” in 1922 in Paris, and the 1926 “2nd International Ladies’ Games” in Göteborg, Sweden."] "\n\n" [:p {} "It was only after these games proved the success of women’s athletics that the IAAF acquiesced and allowed the sport into the Olympics. On 5 April 1926, the IOC capitulated as well, and accepted the recommendation of the IAAF to allow women to compete in track &amp; field athletics at the Olympics. However, in 1928, only five events were held for women, which the FSFI felt was far too few (the men had 21). So, the FSFI also held Women’s World Games in 1930 and 1934, before the organization was absorbed into the IAAF. The " [:a {:href "/editions/9/sports/ATH"} "1928 Olympic track &amp; field events"] " were so few and in such varied disciplines that no single woman could dominate. They were marred when several women finalists were on the verge of collapse after the " [:a {:href "/results/57891"} "800 metres"] ", a not uncommon sight among men either. The IAAF reacted by barring women from running over 200 metres at the Olympics, which was only changed in " [:a {:href "/editions/15/sports/ATH"} "1960"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1928 Olympics had few of the difficulties that would manifest themselves at the next few Games. The depression had not yet occurred, which would mark the " [:a {:href "/editions/10"} "1932 Olympics"] ". Hitler was still in prison and the post-World War II boycotts had not yet occurred. They were missing the single standout athlete and one other thing. Because of illness, Coubertin missed his first Olympics since " [:a {:href "/editions/4"} "1906"] ". He did not get to see women compete in track &amp; field."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Amsterdam (Netherlands) was chosen as the host city at the 20th " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Lausanne, on 2 June 1921, in the dual vote with " [:a {:href "/editions/8"} "Paris for 1924"] ". The only other city considered was Los Angeles, California (United States). Amsterdam had been virtually promised the 1928 Olympics when it stepped aside in favor of Paris for the 1924 Olympic Games, as a favor to " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Baron Pierre de Coubertin"] ", founder of the " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] ", who was retiring as IOC President after the 1924 Olympics and wished to have them held in his native France."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Ceremonies"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped table-condensed"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/officially_opened"} "Offi